  "description": "A parametric DRESSING TABLE on the six-node spine — Geometry → Input Properties → Math Engine → Output Metrics → 2D Render + 3D Render.\n\nTWO MIRROR SHAPES, TWO GEOMETRIES. A rectangular mirror is a quad row like everything else; a round one is a 24-point ring, because it is the one part of this object the quad table cannot express. They are separate slots with separate rows, and \"None\" empties both — so the shape is a real choice rather than a rectangle with rounded corners drawn on it.\n\nTHE MIRROR STANDS ON THE BACK EDGE, not on the object's centre, so a dressing table pushed against a wall puts its mirror on that wall. It is drawn in plan as a thin sliver at the back edge, which is what a plan should show — the mirror is a vertical thing and pretending otherwise clutters the drawing.\n\nKNEE CLEARANCE is published because it is the figure that decides whether a chair fits: the underside of the drawer band, not the underside of the top.",
